The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); RE selection results are consistent (Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}).
Few-NERD provides a notable exception: LP achieves significant gains ($+1.36$~pp, $p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy, while structural signals still fail (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).
CoNLL's near-ceiling single-sample F1 (0.91) with only ${\sim}$4~pp oracle headroom functions primarily as a ceiling-effect control.
Under stringent external TOST anchors ($\delta_0{=}1.0$~pp), some configurations (e.g., Qwen CoNLL) show statistically significant degradation, indicating signals can actively harm selection (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
LP approximately matches greedy decoding ($T{=}0$; $\Delta{=}{+}0.2$~pp, not significant); neither significantly outperforms the other, yet both fall far short of the oracle (0.778, 13.3~pp gap).
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best; closing this gap likely requires trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.\footnote{For negative results (all $p{>}0.05$), uncorrected tests suffice; the sole positive finding (non-tied LP subset, $+2.9$~pp, $p{=}0.004$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) survives Bonferroni correction over 5 comparisons.}

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-dataset comparison of LP selection effectiveness (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Instance degeneracy monotonically predicts LP selection gains across three datasets. Few-NERD uses gold-filtered evaluation ($n{=}32{,}565$); SciERC uses full test set ($n{=}551$) for protocol consistency.\protect\footnotemark}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Headroom & Degen\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 4.0\,pp & 58\% & .316 & fail \\
SciERC & 551 & .614 & 13.8\,pp & 21\% & .205 & $+$0.42\,pp \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 12.4\,pp & 10.1\% & .421 & $\mathbf{+1.36}$\,pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\footnotetext{SciERC greedy (0.614) and headroom (13.8~pp) differ from Table~\ref{tab:selection} (gold-filtered $n{=}529$, greedy${=}0.645$) due to full-set evaluation; the ranking and significance are unchanged.}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) reveals that instance degeneracy monotonically predicts LP selection effectiveness across datasets.
At 10.1\% degeneracy (vs.\ SciERC's 21\%, CoNLL's 58\%), LP achieves highly significant selection gains ($+1.36$~pp, 95\% CI $[+1.18, +1.53]$, $p{<}0.001$), while all other signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$, FK $-2.26$, EM $-3.85$~pp).

\paragraph{Degeneracy as a predictive mechanism.}
The relationship between degeneracy and LP selection is monotonic across the three datasets where selection is informative: 58\% degeneracy (CoNLL) $\to$ LP fails; 21\% (SciERC) $\to$ $+0.42$~pp ($p{=}0.091$); 10.1\% (Few-NERD) $\to$ $+1.36$~pp ($p{<}0.001$).
Low degeneracy ensures that candidates \textit{differ} in F1, providing headroom for LP's per-sample signal to exploit.
The LoRA-rank ablation (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}) corroborates this: reducing rank lowers SciERC degeneracy from 21\% to 7\% and increases oracle headroom, though LP selection still fails there, suggesting that low degeneracy is necessary but interacts with LP compression severity.

\paragraph{The correlation-selection gap persists for structural signals.}
All five signals achieve positive quality estimation on Few-NERD (LP $\rho{=}0.42$, VC $0.39$, FK $0.31$, SJ $0.30$, EM $0.29$), yet only LP translates QE into selection.
The gap between VC's quality estimation ($\rho{=}0.39$) and its selection outcome ($-1.79$~pp) confirms that the gap is not an artifact of weak QE: even strong instance-level QE signals cannot rank candidates within an instance.
Within Few-NERD, the degeneracy-gating pattern holds at entity-type granularity: 7 of 8 types show positive LP selection gains ($+0.23$ to $+4.73$~pp), while the highest-degeneracy type (Art, 19.8\%) is the sole exception (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); LP shows the largest temperature sensitivity ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$).
Signal rankings are stable across seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.0$ at $N{=}8$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}).
NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
